<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Eleanor of Arborea</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1347–<a href="/years/1404">1404</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Eleonora d'Arborea, Eleonora of Arborea</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Eleanor of Arborea was a Sardinian ruler who governed the Judicate of Arborea for her young sons from 1383 until her death in 1404. Daughter of Mariano IV, she continued the war against the Crown of Aragon and in 1392 issued the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carta-de-logu">Carta de Logu</a>, a law code in Sardinian that remained in force across the island until 1827. She is regarded as a Sardinian national heroine.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/eleanor-of-arborea.jpg" alt="Eleanor of Arborea" width="320" height="504" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Antonio Benini · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Elianora_de_Arbar%C3%A8e.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Eleanor, also known as Elianora de Serra Bas, was born around 1347 in Molins de Rei in Catalonia, the daughter of Marianus IV, judge of Arborea, and Timbors of Rocabertí, a Catalan noblewoman. She spent her first years in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oristano">Oristano</a> and at the castle of Goceano. Before 1376 she married Brancaleone Doria, a man of forty from an influential Genoan house that already held lands in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a>, as part of an anti-Aragonese alliance. She lived at Castelgenovese, now Castelsardo, where her sons Fredrick and Marianus were born. In 1382 she lent 4,000 gold florins to Nicolò Guarco, doge of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a>, on terms that tied his daughter Bianchina to her eldest son.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In 1383 her brother Hugh III and his daughter Benedetta were murdered, an act attributed both to Aragon and its allies and to discontent with Hugh's harsh rule and heavy levies for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a>. Eleanor asked King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-iv-of-aragon">Peter IV of Aragon</a> to recognise her son Fredrick as heir and sent Brancaleone to court, where he was held as a hostage. Her brother-in-law Aimery VI of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narbonne">Narbonne</a> claimed the throne for his own son. Back in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oristano">Oristano</a>, Eleanor punished the conspirators and proclaimed herself Judgess under the rules of her grandfather Hugh II, which let women succeed in the absence of male heirs. Peter IV in the end accepted Fredrick as judge, with Eleanor as regent.</p><p>She abandoned her brother's authoritarian methods, defended the judicate's borders and kept the Aragonese penned in a few coastal fortresses while reuniting almost the whole island, as her father had planned. Plague then undid this work, handing much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a> to Aragon without a fight. In her last years she left affairs to her husband and to Marianus V, who had succeeded his brother Fredrick. Tradition holds that she died perhaps of plague, in an unknown place.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Her revision of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carta-de-logu">Carta de Logu</a>, first issued by her father and revised by Hugh III, gave Arborea's laws and institutions a stable form, drawing on Roman-canonical, Byzantine and Bolognese legal traditions as well as Sardinian custom. The scholar Francesco Cesare Casula identified high reliefs in the church of San Gavino Monreale as the only contemporary portraits of Eleanor, Marianus IV, Hugh III and Brancaleone Doria. A statue of her stands in the Piazza Eleonora in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oristano">Oristano</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Eleanor of Arborea” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Carta de Logu, issued by <b>Eleonora of Arborea</b> in the late fourteenth century, remained in force as Sardinian law until 1827, long after Arborea itself had vanished.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-sardinia">Kingdom of Sardinia · At a glance</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Molins de Rei, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. Died in the Judicate of Arborea. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.41,2.02&amp;year=1384" aria-label="Molins de Rei on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4599 687 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Eleanor of Arborea"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5049 957h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5049 957h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.41,2.02&amp;year=1384">What was here in 1384? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
